le" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/state.json" "file_sha256" "422f3aad3a54d06a86a8149634494fbfb38639ec50b964bf61f967a9cd2caf2b"} {"question" "e-13145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/89fa15ffb9a7f6b319cf3b9ff0de4ab821a15f632d7f93618fbe99b7979260f4.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/example-13145.json" "file_sha256" "ad1e155c64b21cebb46bc7894557409c995800e8f8ea74781aa3c4509d085529"} {"question" "e-13145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx \\in \\{1,2,3\\}, \\quad y \\in \\{ -3,-2,-1,0,1,2 \\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad -3 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{ (x,y)  \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z^2}\n\\: : \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}  \n\\leq  x \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3} , \\: \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}  \n\\leq  y \\leq  \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2} \n\\Big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/example-13145.json" "file_sha256" "ad1e155c64b21cebb46bc7894557409c995800e8f8ea74781aa3c4509d085529"} {"question" "e-13183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/787b62fbe367feae9e4c8ed10b983a256840e3a93727fea7c1ff67cacad0129d.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/example-13183.json" "file_sha256" "c1c7c3be0a893c27e2fb422e4fbb313f2c190ce80e0b411e7c433701bd7dd93c"} {"question" "e-13183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x+2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\lt x+2\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z ^2} \\: : \\: y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq }x+2 \\Big\\}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/example-13183.json" "file_sha256" "c1c7c3be0a893c27e2fb422e4fbb313f2c190ce80e0b411e7c433701bd7dd93c"} {"question" "e-13156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/45260087c5196cc239f21402d73169f60139bd7d2c10ee0aa1db4e1a2c56be4a.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/example-13156.json" "file_sha256" "cb2d4e68c7730590ec49800002b49f50d2de158a80b011a06a544b9f6eedf35e"} {"question" "e-13156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\gt 2x, \\qquad y < 3.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y < 3.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y= 3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb R^2 } \\: : \\:  2x \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt } y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq } 3 \\Big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/example-13156.json" "file_sha256" "cb2d4e68c7730590ec49800002b49f50d2de158a80b011a06a544b9f6eedf35e"} {"question" "e-13158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/e5f5f2f2e67ba0ad566b2759ca5536d248f671bf0f082d567a7f7d993772601e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/example-13158.json" "file_sha256" "8eebd04e9f1598b1c7be39aae436396704622376b45ed224f2493840939f9664"} {"question" "e-13158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(10,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\gt 400.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 }x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25 }y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400 \\bigg\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/example-13158.json" "file_sha256" "8eebd04e9f1598b1c7be39aae436396704622376b45ed224f2493840939f9664"}] "usage" {"e-13158" [] "e-13183" [] "q-181678" [] "q-181983" [] "q-181129" [] "e-13156" [] "e-13145" []} "basis" 1518 "retractions" [[17592186198699 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/q-181129.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$R$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}}"] [17592186198732 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/q-181983.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$Z$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [17592186198720 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/q-181678.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$R$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}"] [17592186198079 :question/problem "Describe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/9ec63ab02935633fe11a069f72b4098b.png)"] [17592186198079 :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y$, we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx \\in \\{1,2,3\\}, \\quad y \\in \\{-3,-2,-1,0,1,2 \\}\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad -3 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z^2} \\:: \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1} \\leq x \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3} \\leq y \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2} \\Big\\}\n$$"] [17592186198091 :question/problem "Describe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/592f96a01b0588aaacaa43f58d33b785.png)"] [17592186198091 :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y$, we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\nFor those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\lt x+2\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z ^2} \\:: \\: y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}x+2 \\Big\\}\n$$"] [17592186198080 :question/problem "Describe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/7cac8ef29134e3864a40c2aac8a76339.png)"] [17592186198080 :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=3$.\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\gt 2x, \\qquad y < 3\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y < 3\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y= 3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} 3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb R^2} \\:: \\: 2x \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt} y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} 3 \\Big\\}\n$$"] [17592186198081 :question/problem "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/fecea6c55850b5b4d4d702f2d38d5315.png)"] [17592186198081 :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{25} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(10,0)$. Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 \\gt 400\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400\n$$\n\nsince the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq} 400\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\:: \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16}x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25}y^2 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq} 400 \\bigg\\}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186195145 17592186195146 17592186195147 17592186195148 17592186195150 17592186195151 17592186195152 17592186195153 17592186195155 17592186195156 17592186195157 17592186195158 17592186195160 17592186195161 17592186195162 17592186195163 17592186195165 17592186195166 17592186195167 17592186195168 17592186195169 17592186195170 17592186195325 17592186195326 17592186195327 17592186195328 17592186195329 17592186195331 17592186195332 17592186195333 17592186195334 17592186195335 17592186195337 17592186195338 17592186195339 17592186195340 17592186195341 17592186195442 17592186195443 17592186195444 17592186195445 17592186195446 17592186195447 17592186195449 17592186195450 17592186195451 17592186195452 17592186195453] "immutable_fields" [[17592186195144 :answer-field/id] [17592186195144 :answer-field/key] [1759